TrivialChat example illustrates how LJMS can be used in applet context.

Although it's funcionality is very primitive (as it's name hints), it could be taken as the base
for any complexity chat application with any business logic: one can extend MessageServlet
and MessageContext for the server part of the broker adding necessary data-structures
(Collections etc - as users/clients views) - if needed. 
Notice: TrivialApplet example does not extend MessageServlet class: it uses the default built-in
Collection from that class (all users sessions collection), extending only MessageContext
(default router - MessageContext - does nothing with messages on the server side), 
so for trivial chat functionality we had at least to broadcast messages
to all the clients (which is done by means of broadcast() built-in method). 
Less trivial chatServers probably will define their own views of clients (probably in multiple
Collections: groups of users, different categories of users etc) in MessageContext subclasses
and will add/remove them (to those collections) from MessageServlet subclass 
(see other examples illustrating this).
The main rule: when user connects, single instance of MessageServlet (or it's subclass) 
will be created on the server for it. The common structure (context) visible for all such servlets
is MessageContext which plays the role of wire or glue, connecting all MessageServlet instances.

This example uses LSessionRunnableImpl class (threaded version of session),
which means that session has built-in thread and it calling back message/error
listeners (without it - broker does nothing), in our case - applet class.
See app2app example how LSessionImpl class (not threaded session) is used
for synchronius message delivery (in user application thread).


To run example:
1.cd to the example directory;
2.start runServer.sh under Unix or runServer.bat under Win on the same machine where you have a running web-server.
3.put index.html, images directory and applet.jar under the root of the web-server (or alternatively run appletviewer)
4.point a browser to index.html

For brevity necessary code snippets from this example are here:

SERVER:
      Servlet server=new org.is.server.MessageServlet(new TrivialChatRouter());
      server.startService(port);


CLIENT:

    LSessionRunnableImpl messageCentral;
    
    //INITIALIZE AND START THE BROKER:
    try{
      messageCentral=new LSessionRunnableImpl();
      messageCentral.setMessageTransport(new MessageSocket(host, port));
      messageCentral.setMessageListener(this);
      messageCentral.setExceptionListener(this); //if not set - printing to console
      messageCentral.connect();                  //make connection (init)
      messageCentral.start();                    //start message delivery
    }
    catch(JMSException e){
      System.err.println("Error connecting:"+e);
    }
    
    ....
    //SOMEWHERE IN THE CODE:
    sendStringMessage(input.getText()); //caused by GUI thread
    ....
    
    private void sendStringMessage(String str){
  
     try {
       Message ms=new MessageImpl();
       ms.setStringProperty("text",str);
       messageCentral.sendMessage(ms);
     }
     catch (Exception e) {
       System.out.println("Can't send message: " + e);
     }
    }
    
    //FREE RESOURCES (close session at the end of the work with broker):
    public void stop(){

     try{
       messageCentral.close();
     }
     catch(Exception e){
     }
    }    